{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>וַתִּרְאֵהוּ אֶת הַיֶּלֶד וְגוֹ׳</b> — it requires a fine point to examine in the verse's saying <b>וַתִּרְאֵהוּ</b> etc., for it should have said וַתֵּרֶא וְגוֹ׳. And it appears that it intended to say that she knew the ark was one of the children cast into the Nile, and she intended to save him, as we explained in the verse ״וַתִּשְׁלַח אֶת אֲמָתָהּ,״ and that is the meaning of its saying <b>וַתִּפְתַּח וַתִּרְאֵהוּ</b> — the child she intended to save. For if the verse had said וַתִּרְאֶה יֶלֶד, the implication would be that she saw something she had not so intended. Understand this." "And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshaned (Sotah 12b): ״וַתִּרְאֵהוּ״ — she saw the Shekhinah with him. And this is the way of derash. But one must say that she herself did not recognize the Shekhinah, for from where would she have known the Shekhinah from the house of her father and her father's father? Rather, she saw with him a great light, and HaShem did so in order to magnify the child's merit in her eyes. Also according to the reading that our Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshaned (Zohar II:12b) in the verse ״וְהִנֵּה נַעַר בֹּכֶה״ — that it alluded to Israel, who were weeping in exile — therefore HaShem uncovered her eyes to show her the light attending Moses." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that her sensation of the Shekhinah was on account of the fact that when she touched him, tzara'at flew off from her (Exodus Rabbah 1:23); she said: This can only be a divine child. And perhaps the flight of the tzara'at is alluded to in its saying <b>וַתִּרְאֵהוּ אֶת הַיֶּלֶד</b> — meaning, וַתִּרְאֵהוּ in the sense stated nearby in potential, for it is written ״לִרְחוֹץ״ and the remembrance of ״בְּשָׂרָהּ״ is in potential; and when she opened the ark and saw — her flesh, the child — meaning, when she touched the child — <b>וְהִנֵּה נַעַר</b> — meaning, he reverted to being like the flesh of a young boy, after the manner of what the verse states regarding Naaman (II Kings 5:14): ״וַיָּשָׁב בְּשָׂרוֹ כִּבְשַׂר נַעַר קָטֹן״." "<b>וַתַּחְמֹל עָלָיו.</b> According to what we explained — that she intended from the outset and at first to save the child — the reason for the compassion is a new one: not to save him from the Nile, but the verse tells that she sought to nurse him. And the proof is what it says immediately afterward: ״וַתֹּאמֶר אֲחֹתוֹ וְגוֹ׳ הַאֵלֵךְ וְקָרָאתִי״ וְגוֹ׳, and our Rabbis, of blessed memory (Sotah 12b), said that she returned him to wet-nurses, non-Jewish women, etc." "<b>וַתֹּאמֶר מִיַּלְדֵי הָעִבְרִים וְגוֹ׳.</b> It is difficult: what novelty is she expressing by saying <b>מִיַּלְדֵי הָעִבְרִים זֶה</b>? It is self-evident that this is so, for they — the women — are the ones casting their children into the Nile by decree of Pharaoh. And if according to what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, darshaned (Sotah 12a) — that on that same day Pharaoh decreed also upon the Egyptians — if so, from where did she know and recognize that he was not from the children of the Egyptians? And moreover, according to what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said — that she returned him to several non-Jewish wet-nurses and he would not nurse — this is a sign for you that he was not from the children of the Egyptian women. In any case, what novelty is she expressing in her words?" "To grasp the matter, let us examine whether her saving the child was because she was in doubt whether he was Egyptian, or perhaps even if he were Hebrew. Now, if we say that she thought to save a possible Egyptian, she intended in her saying <b>from the children of the Hebrews — this one</b> because the opposite of what she thought became clear to her, and thereby she revealed her mind — that she had not intended to save one of the children of the Hebrews. And this line of reasoning one would choose, for an ordinary gentile woman would have compassion only on the children of her own nation. And even though we see that after she knew he was from the children of the Hebrews she took hold of him and hired a wet nurse for him, this is no proof, for after she knew his greatness — and also that he healed her from her leprosy — she had compassion on him; and he is worthy, by virtue of this, to incline her heart toward the faith of Israel, for she saw how awe-inspiring are His deeds." "<b>And if</b> we say that from the start she had compassion on the child, even though she knew he was of the children of Israel, she intended in her saying <b>from the children of the Hebrews</b> to give the reason that he did not nurse from the Egyptian women — not that he was weakened by the river and was in danger and unable to nurse, but rather because he was from the children of the Hebrews. And for this reason his sister immediately said to her, <b>Shall I go and call for you</b> etc. — from the Hebrew women — to confirm her words. And Scripture says <b>and the woman took</b> etc. <b>and nursed him.</b>"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {2 "The phrase לְהָאָמוּר בְּסָמוּךְ בְּכֹחַ refers to Leviticus 13:6 ('לִרְחוֹץ') and 13:39 ('בְּשָׂרָהּ'), where the Or HaChaim reads the potential for tzara'at skin imagery latent in those nearby terms and applies it to the surface of Pharaoh's daughter's flesh upon contact with the child."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:23:55.808333901Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}